# Vulnerabilities accepted, for a bounded time, in the Gotenberg image pinned by
# docker-compose.gotenberg.yml. Gate: script/gates/cve_accepted_diff.sh, run nightly by
# .github/workflows/gotenberg-cve.yml.
#
# Format, one record per line:
#
#     ADVISORY-ID | valid-through (YYYY-MM-DD) | why it is accepted
#
# Blank lines and `#` lines are ignored. Every field is validated and a malformed record
# FAILS the gate rather than being skipped: an acceptance whose date does not parse is an
# acceptance that never expires, and this project already has a rule about things that
# never expire.
#
# --- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The scan was `trivy --exit-code 1` first, and on its first real run it went red and
# STAYED red for a reason it had no way to say. MEASURED 2026-08-10: the pinned image has
# five fixable HIGH findings across four advisories, and `gotenberg/gotenberg:8` and
# `gotenberg/gotenberg:8.35.0` BOTH resolve to the digest already pinned — 8.35.0 being
# the newest tag upstream publishes. The workflow's own remediation ("move the pin")
# named an action that did not exist.
#
# A gate nobody can satisfy gets switched off, and a gate switched off is worse than no
# gate — INV-4. Making it advisory is forbidden in the other direction (CLAUDE.md §7). So
# the gate stays a gate and the exceptions are enumerated here, each dated, each with a
# reason. What the gate then refuses is:
#
#   1. a fixable HIGH/CRITICAL this file does not name  — a new vulnerability
#   2. an entry whose valid-through date has passed     — an acceptance nobody re-examined
#   3. an entry the scan no longer reports              — a stale exemption; delete it
#   4. a malformed record — an id that could not have come off a scan, a date that is not
#      a real day, no reason, or an expiry more than 90 days out
#
# (2) is HARD in the nightly scan and in any change touching this file, the compose pin or
# the gate. It is a WARNING in `ci.yml`'s `gates` job, which runs on every branch: a hard
# expiry there turns the morning after a date passes into a red build on work that cannot
# fix it, and the cheapest unblock would be a date bump nobody thought about. (4) is hard
# everywhere.
#
# THE 90-DAY CEILING IS THE GATE'S, not a convention. `2099-12-31 | reason` is a permanent
# exemption wearing a date, and requiring an expiry to be PRESENT does not bound it.
#
# (3) is what stops this file becoming permanent furniture, and it is the same assertion
# zero_reporter.allowlist above it lives under: THIS LIST MAY ONLY SHRINK, except when a
# newly published vulnerability is consciously accepted with a date.
#
# --- WHAT `valid-through` MEANS -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not a prediction of when a fix lands. It is the date somebody must LOOK AGAIN: has
# upstream rebuilt, and is the reason on the record still true? Thirty days makes that a
# nightly build going red once a month rather than a calendar entry nobody keeps. The gate
# will not accept more than 90.
#
# ======================================================================================
# Gotenberg 8.37.0 — digest f29984bd1e226bf1b93ba90af06000afa8b315853e99d27b9aaa41b93f15c769
# ======================================================================================
#
# NO ACCEPTANCES. The three records accepted against 8.35.0 (digest a16a14e1f18a…) expired
# on 2026-09-09 and were not renewed: Jan decided on 2026-10-08 to move the pin to a release
# that carries the fixes rather than extend them, and Gotenberg 8.37.0 does. Measured with
# Trivy 0.73.0 (HIGH,CRITICAL, --ignore-unfixed, database of 2026-10-08):
#
#   CVE-2026-19155  chromium 151.0.7922.71   -> 8.37.0 ships chromium 152.0.7977.82: absent
#   CVE-2026-46602  pdfcpu golang.org/x/image -> absent
#   CVE-2026-56852  pdfcpu golang.org/x/text  -> absent
#
# CVE-2026-46604 (x/image, TIFF panic) was accepted on 2026-08-10 and deleted on 2026-08-11
# when the database stopped listing it for the same digest. If a scan reports it again, the
# database has listed it again; it is not a new finding nobody has seen.
#
# Keeping them would have been the stale arm's case (3): an exemption for something the scan
# no longer reports. They are history now, not exemptions.
#
# ACCEPTED UNTIL 2026-12-11, BY JAN'S DECISION OF 2026-10-12 (round 6, Q11, option b).
#
# 157 fixable HIGH/CRITICAL advisories, which is every id a Trivy 0.73.0 scan of this digest
# reports with the database of 2026-10-12 (HIGH,CRITICAL, --ignore-unfixed, --scanners vuln).
# The decision named 136, the count on 2026-10-08's database; between those dates one id
# dropped off (CVE-2026-75804, so it is not listed) and 22 of the same kind were added, and
# "accept them all" has to cover those for the workflow's comparison to pass. Most are
# Chromium 152 with fixes in 153/154; the rest are Debian packages (perl, python3.13, glib,
# pcre2, sqlite and others) and Go stdlib / golang.org/x/net in the gotenberg and pdfcpu
# binaries. No published Gotenberg release carries the fixes.
#
# THE REASON IS THE CONTAINMENT, and it is the same for every line, which is why it is
# written once here and named at the end of each record: the documented compose file gives
# the container no route off its network (`internal: true`), runs it non-root with Chromium's
# own sandbox left ON, a read-only root filesystem, `cap_drop: ALL` and `no-new-privileges`,
# and starts it with the LibreOffice and PDF-engine routes disabled. That decides what an
# attacker reaches after an exploit; it does not fix one.
#
# 60 DAYS, not the 30 this file usually uses: Jan's choice. Re-check, and move the pin, as
# soon as Gotenberg publishes a release with Chromium 154; every line that the next scan no
# longer reports must then be deleted (stale arm).

CVE-2026-8376 | 2026-12-11 | libperl5.40 5.40.1-6 (debian layer), fixed in 5.40.1-6+deb13u1; perl 5.40.1-6 (debian layer), fixed in 5.40.1-6+deb13u1; perl-base 5.40.1-6 (debian layer), fixed in 5.40.1-6+deb13u1; perl-modules-5.40 5.40.1-6 (debian layer), fixed in 5.40.1-6+deb13u1. No Gotenberg release carries the fix; contained: no network, non-root, Chromium sandbox on, PDF-engine routes off.
